{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"In SCOP 1.75, PDB 1acc chain A (anthrax protective antigen, 735 residues, UniProt P13423) was classified as one domain, d1acca_ (sunid 43828, f.11.1.1, 'Anthrax protective antigen'). Its record reads '1acc A:' with no residue range, so the whole chain was treated as a single unit. In the current SCOPe 2.08 stable release, the same chain is split into two domains: d1acca2 (A:14-225, b.179.1.1, PA14 family, fold b.179 'Anthrax protective antigen N-terminal-like', class b 'All beta proteins') and d1acca1 (A:226-735, f.60.1.1, 'Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like', fold f.60, class f). So the change is a re-partitioning into two modules with different class, fold, and family assignments. The C-terminal module moved from f.11 to f.60, and the N-terminal segment became a separate beta-class PA14 domain. The 2.08 record does not give the SCOP 1.75 identifier forward: d1acca_ does not exist as d1acca1 or d1acca2, and direct lookup of d1acca1 in release 1.75 returned 404.

What it reveals about domains: a SCOP domain is a classification unit meant to capture a compact, independently folding evolutionary module, not automatically a whole polypeptide chain. A multi-module chain can be labeled as one domain in an older release, so its single classification describes at most one part of the chain. Domain boundaries and classifications are curatorial judgments that are revised as structural evidence accumulates. The records show that the assignment changed; they do not by themselves prove that the two modules evolved independently of each other, or that the old f.11 label was wrong at the level of the whole evolutionary history of the protein.

Homology-search implications: (1) Do not carry the 1.75 whole-chain label f.11.1.1 forward to the full 735-residue chain. Use 2.08 per-domain assignments, and search or compare the N-terminal (14-225) and C-terminal (226-735) regions as separate queries or templates. (2) A hit that covers only one module supports only that module's lineage; the other module's classification is not implied. (3) Old whole-chain annotations, or a library built from 1.75, may give partial coverage and lower scores for either module, so per-region support should be checked. (4) Homology should be inferred from sequence or structure evidence for each module. Shared naming ('Anthrax protective antigen') reflects naming continuity, not demonstrated homology between the PA14-like and f.60 modules, and a shared chain does not establish shared fold or family. (5) Pin the SCOPe release in analyses and map annotations by PDB chain and residue range or UniProt accession, not by SID, because SIDs change across releases. (6) The 2.08 chain record lists 3tewA as a cluster representative at 96.9% sequence identity. That is sequence similarity to a representative, not structural evidence, and should not be used as a template without checking. Limits: I used the default 2.08 stable release and did not query the 2.08-2026-08-16 periodic-update label. I did not run sequence or structure hit searches, so this answer does not establish any external homolog, function, or domain count for 1acc.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry (release 2.08 and 1.75)","mcp__scope__get_scop_chain (1accA, release 2.08)","mcp__scope__search_scop (identifier 1acc, release 2.08)","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains (d1acca1, d1acca2 in 2.08; d1acca_ in 1.75; d1acca1/d1acca2 in 1.75 returned 404)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sunid 43828, release 1.75)","/api/v1/releases/1.75/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/chains/1accA","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/parents","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_/parents","/api/v1/releases/1.75/sunids/43828/parents"]}]}
